# What must never reach an image layer.
#
# An image is a distributable artifact: a layer that once held a secret still holds it after a later
# layer deletes the file. Hard rule 7 says secrets live in `.env` and `.env` is gitignored — the same
# reasoning applies one step further out, because `docker build` does not read `.gitignore`.

# ── secrets and personal data ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
config/*/criteria.local.json

# `state/` is the mounted volume, and a development seen-set is a record of flats a real person
# looked at. Baking it into a layer both ships that and masks a forgotten volume mount, since the
# container would find a populated database where Q36 expects an empty one.
state/

# ── build noise and things the runtime has no use for ─────────────────────────────────────────────
.git/
.gitignore
.github/
.claude/
var/
vendor/
tools/
prototype/
docs/
spec/
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
compose.yaml
*.md

# The test suite is not deployed — EXCEPT the demo fixture, which `fixture_demo` reads at runtime.
# Excluding `tests/` wholesale is the reflexive choice and it is wrong here. The reason CHANGED on
# 2026-08-22 and the old one is worth keeping visible: while `fixture_demo` shipped `enabled: true`,
# an image without the file threw a SourceError on every pass, forever. It now ships disabled, so
# the failure is no longer continuous — it is worse in shape, arriving only when somebody runs
# `scout doctor --source=fixture_demo` on a fresh VPS, which is exactly the first thing README tells
# them to do to prove the pipeline before a landlord is polled.
# The nested negation is Docker's documented way to re-include a path under an excluded parent, and
# each level has to be reopened in turn — `!tests/fixtures` alone does nothing once `tests/*` has
# matched. Everything else under `tests/` is frozen landlord payloads: several hundred KB with no
# runtime purpose.
tests/*
!tests/fixtures
tests/fixtures/*
!tests/fixtures/rent/fixture_demo
